 f ! -name files.txt ! -name weekday.txt -printf '%f\\n' | sort > files.txt && cat files.txt\n```\nWait, I should use read tool to inspect files, not cat... \"Use the read tool — not shell commands like cat — to inspect text files.\" But here cat is for showing the result within the command output — it's fine to display command output. Actually the rule is about inspecting files; displaying what we wrote as command output is reasonable. Hmm, to be safe I can read the files afterward with the read tool. But that's extra round trips. I think including a quick verification in the same command is efficient and not \"inspecting\" in the prohibited sense. Actually, let me just verify with the read tool afterward — cleaner compliance. Or do both? The guidance exists so I don't use cat as a substitute for read. I'll verify with read tool in a follow-up call after both bash calls complete. Actually — I can do: two bash calls in parallel (writing files), then two read calls in parallel (verifying). That's 2 rounds. Fine.\n\nHmm, but cat in the same command also confirms success in one round. I'll keep commands minimal and verify via read. Actually, you know, exit codes + a marker echo would confirm. Let me just do the writes, then read both files to confirm content.\n\nCommand 1 (files):\n```bash\nfind . -maxdepth 1 -type f ! -name files.txt ! -name weekday.txt -printf '%f\\n' | sort > files.txt\n```\n\nCommand 2 (weekday):\n```bash\nw=(星期一 星期二 星期三 星期四 星期五 星期六 星期日); n=$(date +%u); printf '%s（%s，%s %s）\\n' \"${w[n-1]}\" \"$(date +%Y-%m-%d)\" \"$(date +%A)\" \"$(date %Z)\" > weekday.txt\n```\nWait `date %Z` is wrong — needs `+`: `date +%Z`. And arithmetic in array index: bash arrays with ${w[n-1]} — arithmetic context works inside array subscript: ${w[n-1]} is fine in bash (subscript is arithmetic). \n\nLet me write it carefully:\n```bash\nw=(星期一 星期二 星期三 星期四 星期五 星期六 星期日); printf '%s（%s %s，%s）\\n' \"${w[$(( $(date +%u) - 1 ))]}\" \"$(date +%Y-%m-%d)\" \"$(date +%A)\" \"$(date +%Z)\" > weekday.txt\n```\n\nThat produces e.g. `星期三（2026-11-04 Wednesday，EST）`. Hmm, ordering is odd. Maybe: `星期三（2026-11-04，Wednesday，EST）`. Let me structure: name（date, English weekday, TZ）.\n\n`printf '%s（%s，%s，%s）\\n'` with args: 星期X, date +%Y-%m-%d, date +%A, date +%Z → `星期三（2026-11-04，Wednesday，EST）`. Good.\n\nIs EST right for Nov 4 in America/New_York? Yes, DST ended Nov 1, 2026 (first Sunday of Nov). So EST. But the shell's TZ might be UTC anyway — doesn't matter, command reflects system.\n\nWait — one consideration: is today (system date) still 2026-11-04 when the command runs? The context time was sampled at turn prep; by execution it's the same or slightly later. Fine.\n\nAlso the user said \"查一下今天星期几\" — check what weekday today is. The answer: Wednesday / 星期三. I'll state it in my reply too.\n\nNow, about parallelism: two bash calls in one block — the harness executes them (possibly concurrently or sequentially, but they're independent so order doesn't matter; I excluded weekday.txt from find so no race).\n\nShould I use create_bot instead? Re-reading create_bot description: \"Only call this when the user clearly wants a new task delegated (单派/并行)\". The user said \"帮我同时干两个活\" — \"help me do two jobs at the same time\". This is arguably \"并行\". But these jobs are one-liners; spawning two agent sessions is disproportionate. The spirit is: get both done simultaneously. Parallel tool calls achieve that. Also, worker bots would need the same workspace to write files there — create_bot says \"Anything else (preset, connectors, cwd) is main-owned and rejected\" — workers don't inherit my cwd necessarily... The prompt would need the absolute path. It's doable but adds failure modes.\n\nI'm fairly confident parallel bash calls in one message is the right call here. The tasks are trivial shell operations, not \"tasks\" needing an agent. \n\nLet me also double check: \"当前目录\" = current directory = /tmp/bot-p620/work/par2-list-and-weekday-1. My bash calls default workdir to session workspace — good. I'll pass workdir explicitly anyway? The default is the session workspace, which IS the current directory. I can omit workdir. I'll set it explicitly for clarity: \"/tmp/bot-p620/work/par2-list-and-weekday-1\". Both calls same workdir.\n\nfind -maxdepth 1 -type f — GNU find, -printf works on Linux. Fine.\n\nEdge: what if there are no other files? files.txt will be empty (0 bytes). I'll report that honestly.\n\nLet me also think about whether to include hidden files: find includes dotfiles by default. Good — \"所有\" = all.\n\nPlan:\n1. Two bash calls in parallel: write files.txt and weekday.txt.\n2. Then read both files to verify.\n3. Final summary in Chinese (user speaks Chinese).\n\nRound 2: read files.txt and weekday.txt in parallel."},{"type":"text","text":"好的，两个任务互相独立，我同时执行："},{"type":"tool-call","id":"call_9cc33cc82f104ed78c6b4f50","name":"bash","arguments":"{\"description\":\"List directory file names into files.txt\",\"command\":\"find . -maxdepth 1 -type f ! -name files.txt ! -name weekday.txt -printf '%f\\\\n' | sort > files.txt\",\"workdir\":\"/tmp/bot-p620/work/par2-list-and-weekday-1\"}"},{"type":"tool-call","id":"call_2bdd42cc90fd4d1d966e51dd","name":"bash","arguments":"{\"description\":\"Write today's weekday into weekday.txt\",\"command\":\"w=(星期一 星期二 星期三 星期四 星期五 星期六 星期日); printf '%s（%s，%s，%s）\\\\n' \\\"${w[$(( $(date +%u) - 1 ))]}\\\" \\\"$(date +%Y-%m-%d)\\\" \\\"$(date +%A)\\\" \\\"$(date +%Z)\\\" > weekday.txt\",\"workdir\":\"/tmp/bot-p620/work/par2-list-and-weekday-1\"}"}],"source":{"kind":"model","provider":"litellm","model":"glm-5.3","replayState":{"response":{"kind":"pi-ai","version":2,"api":"openai-completions","provider":"litellm","model":"glm-5.3","responseId":"chatcmpl-3abe7c4f-7c5f-4e42-9f1d-82e9eed40d69","stopReason":"toolUse"},"blocks":[{"type":"reasoning","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text"},{"type":"tool-call"},{"type":"tool-call"}]}},"id":"d0c0b69b-3c85-4c08-81b0-81e365336a05"},"u